  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:power-management",
  "domain": "robotics",
  "definition": "Power management is the discipline of regulating, distributing, monitoring, and optimising electrical energy flow within computing, robotic, and embedded systems to maximise operational endurance, thermal safety, and efficiency. It spans hardware circuits (DC\u2013DC converters, power distribution units, battery management systems), firmware-level control algorithms (voltage-frequency scaling, clock-gating, duty-cycle regulation), and system-level policies (workload scheduling, energy-aware task allocation) that together balance instantaneous demand against available supply. In robotic and mobile contexts it additionally encompasses energy harvesting, regenerative braking, and state-of-charge estimation to enable untethered autonomous operation. Effective power management is a prerequisite for deploying autonomous systems in resource-constrained field environments and for meeting safety standards governing collaborative human\u2013robot interaction.",
